  "summary": "Decorative glazed ceramic tiles used extensively in Portuguese and Spanish architecture.",
  "description": "Characterized by intricate blue and white patterns, later expanding to polychrome designs; developed through Islamic influence and Spanish techniques, becoming integral to Iberian architectural identity.",
  "culturalContext": "The azulejo — Portugal's painted tin-glazed tile — turned the whole country into a ceramic museum after Moorish az-zulayj met Flemish and Italian techniques: 16th-century blue-and-white Delft-competitors, 18th-century polychrome story panels (churches, convents), and 20th-century modernism (Almada Negreiros's São Paulo station panels). Igreja facades, metro walls, boulevard storefronts: Lisbon and Porto are tile cities, and the National Tile Museum in Lisbon preserves 500 years of the craft's grammar.",
